+// RIPWIRE_VENDOR_PATCH(markdown/002-counter-saturate): every counter this scanner accumulates into
+// is a uint8_t — serialize() writes each as a single byte — and upstream feeds them with a bare
+// `+=` from advance()'s size_t. Past 255 that is an implicit truncating conversion, and G1 is
+// -fno-sanitize-recover=all, so it aborts the run rather than warning. Trigger in the wild:
+// rails/guides/source/getting_started.md, a pipe-table row padded to 301 columns. Tabs reach it
+// far sooner than spaces do — advance() charges a tab at tab stop 4, so 64 tabs is enough.
+//
+// SATURATE, do not cast. Both counters are read by ORDERING tests against a FIXED threshold:
+// indentation by `>= 4` (indented chunk), `< 4` (thematic break) and `< list_item_indentation(
+// block )` (max 17); the fence `level` by `>= 3` before a fence may open at all. 255 answers every
+// one of those exactly as any larger true value would, so saturating is not an approximation here.
+// Wrapping is: it does not blur those predicates, it INVERTS them, and only inside a narrow window
+// that makes the bug easy to miss. Measured, `# Buried` under N columns and a fence of N marks:
+//
+//   N=255  parses correctly    N=256/257  WRONG, at exit 0    N=300  correct again, by luck
+//
+// because 300 - 256 = 44 is still >= 4 and >= 3. The damage window is N mod 256 in 0..3, which is
+// why a 300-wide fixture reproduces the sanitizer abort while asserting nothing about the parse.
+// At 256 an indented code block came back as a heading, and a fence never opened so its contents
+// leaked out as live markdown — ripwire emitted phantom symbols for both, at exit 0.
+//
+// counter_saturating_add is the implementation from the parallel lane on
+// claude/amazing-wescoff-181432, which found this semantics argument and the fixture-width trap;
+// renamed from their indent_saturating_add only because it now also serves the fence counter.
+// <stdint.h> arrives via src/tree_sitter/parser.h, so no new include.
+//
+// FOUR SITES ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT BARE, because wrapping is unreachable at each and a smaller
+// diff re-applies more cleanly across an upstream bump:
+//   * match()/INDENTED_CODE_BLOCK — loop guard `while( s->indentation < 4 )`, advance() returns at
+//     most 4, so the counter cannot exceed 7.
+//   * match()/LIST_ITEM..LIST_ITEM_MAX_INDENTATION — guard `< list_item_indentation( block )`,
+//     itself at most 17, so at most ~21.
+//   * match()/BLOCK_QUOTE and parse_block_quote() — a single `advance( … ) - 1` onto a counter the
+//     line above sets to 0, so at most 3.
+// The sibling patches rust/001, lua/001 and csharp/001 keep an explicit CAST instead: their
+// counters close a token by matching the opening count, saturation is not more correct there, and
+// measurement found no extraction difference at any width (255/256/257/300 all recover the symbols
+// after the token), so those three contribute nothing to kParserVer.
+static uint8_t counter_saturating_add(uint8_t counter, size_t width) {
+    return (size_t)counter + width > UINT8_MAX
+               ? (uint8_t)UINT8_MAX
+               : (uint8_t)(counter + width);
+}
